    <fr:title text="A Map with an Inverse Is an Equivalence">A Map with an Inverse Is an Equivalence</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Proposition</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter><html:p>
    For every map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex>, a <fr:link href="/def-005I/" title="Map with an Inverse" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005I/" display-uri="def-005I" type="local">map with an inverse</fr:link> determines
    an <fr:link href="/def-005C/" title="Equivalence of Types" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005C/" display-uri="def-005C" type="local">equivalence</fr:link>. Explicitly, there is a canonical function
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {hasInverse}(f)\to \mathsf {isEquiv}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
 
   
   <fr:tree show-metadata="false" toc="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:taxon>Proof</fr:taxon></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(g,G,H):\mathsf {hasInverse}(f)]]></fr:tex>. By definition,
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        G:f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B,
        \qquad 
        H:g\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A.
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Therefore <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(g,G):\mathsf {sec}(f)]]></fr:tex> and
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(g,H):\mathsf {retr}(f)]]></fr:tex>. Pairing them gives
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \bigl ((g,G),(g,H)\bigr )
        :\mathsf {sec}(f)\times \mathsf {retr}(f)
        :\equiv \mathsf {isEquiv}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
 
</fr:mainmatter>
