                  <fr:month>7</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:title text="Homotopies">Homotopies</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0059/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>def-0059</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/def-0059/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Homotopy">Homotopy</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B:A\to \mathcal {U}]]></fr:tex> be a type family and let
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f,g:\Pi _{x:A}B(x)]]></fr:tex> be dependent functions. A <html:strong>homotopy</html:strong> from
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex> is a pointwise identification between their values. Its type is
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        f\sim  g\;:\equiv \;\Pi _{x:A}\bigl (f(x)=g(x)\bigr ).
    ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <html:p>
        Homotopies express commutative diagrams in type theory. They have reflexivity, inverse, and
        concatenation operations inherited pointwise from identity types.
    </html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>7</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:title text="Bi-invertible maps">Bi-invertible maps</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-005G/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>def-005G</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/def-005G/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Section of a Map">Section of a Map</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex>. A <html:strong>section</html:strong> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> is a map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g:B\to  A]]></fr:tex>
    equipped with a <fr:link href="/def-0059/" title="Homotopy" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-0059/" display-uri="def-0059" type="local">homotopy</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B]]></fr:tex>.
    The type of sections of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> is
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {sec}(f)
        :\equiv 
        \Sigma _{g:B\to  A}\bigl (f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B\bigr ).
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Thus a section of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> is a right inverse of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> up to homotopy.
</html:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-005H/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>def-005H</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/def-005H/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Retraction of a Map">Retraction of a Map</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex>. A <html:strong>retraction</html:strong> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> is a map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h:B\to  A]]></fr:tex>
    equipped with a <fr:link href="/def-0059/" title="Homotopy" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-0059/" display-uri="def-0059" type="local">homotopy</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A]]></fr:tex>.
    The type of retractions of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> is
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {retr}(f)
        :\equiv 
        \Sigma _{h:B\to  A}\bigl (h\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A\bigr ).
    ]]></fr:tex>
    If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> has a retraction, then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is called a retract of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-005B/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>def-005B</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/def-005B/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Bi-invertible Map">Bi-invertible Map</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <html:p>
    A map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex> is <html:strong>bi-invertible</html:strong> if it has both a
    <fr:link href="/def-005G/" title="Section of a Map" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005G/" display-uri="def-005G" type="local">section</fr:link> and a <fr:link href="/def-005H/" title="Retraction of a Map" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005H/" display-uri="def-005H" type="local">retraction</fr:link>, or equivalently a separate
    <fr:link href="/def-005A/" title="Left and Right Inverses" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005A/" display-uri="def-005A" type="local">right and left inverse</fr:link>. Define
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {isBiInv}(f)
        :\equiv 
        \mathsf {sec}(f)\times \mathsf {retr}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex>
    The left and right inverse maps are supplied independently; their inverse
    laws imply that they are homotopic.
</html:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-005C/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>def-005C</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/def-005C/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Equivalence of Types">Equivalence of Types</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <html:p>
    An <html:strong>equivalence</html:strong> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> is a map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex> equipped
    with evidence that it is <fr:link href="/def-005B/" title="Bi-invertible Map" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005B/" display-uri="def-005B" type="local">bi-invertible</fr:link>. The type of equivalences
    is
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {isEquiv}(f):\equiv \mathsf {isBiInv}(f),\qquad 
        A\simeq  B
        \;:\equiv \;
        \Sigma _{f:A\to  B}\mathsf {isEquiv}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex>
    We write <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e:A\simeq  B]]></fr:tex> for an equivalence and use its first projection as
    the underlying map.
</html:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-005I/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>def-005I</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/def-005I/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Map with an Inverse">Map with an Inverse</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex>. We say that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> <html:strong>has an inverse</html:strong> if there is a
    single map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g:B\to  A]]></fr:tex> that is both a right inverse and a left inverse of
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex>, up to homotopy. Define
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {hasInverse}(f)
        :\equiv 
        \Sigma _{g:B\to  A}
        \bigl (f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B\bigr )
        \times 
        \bigl (g\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A\bigr ).
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Thus an element of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {hasInverse}(f)]]></fr:tex> is a triple <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(g,G,H)]]></fr:tex>, where
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G:f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B]]></fr:tex> and
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[H:g\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <html:p>
        It is tempting to define an equivalence using a single inverse. The problem is that
        <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {hasInverse}(f)]]></fr:tex> can contain nontrivial higher information, whereas
        <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {isEquiv}(f)]]></fr:tex> will be shown, using function extensionality, to be a proposition.
        For example, a later calculation gives
        <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {hasInverse}(\mathsf {id}_{\mathbb {S}^1})\simeq \mathbb {Z}]]></fr:tex>.
    </html:p>
                <html:p>
        Consequently, the following two constructions establish logical equivalence, but they do
        not assert that the witness types <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {hasInverse}(f)]]></fr:tex> and
        <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {isEquiv}(f)]]></fr:tex> are themselves equivalent. The composites of the two constructions
        need not be homotopic to the corresponding identity maps.
    </html:p>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0015/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>thm-0015</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/thm-0015/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="A Map with an Inverse Is an Equivalence">A Map with an Inverse Is an Equivalence</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Proposition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter><html:p>
    For every map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex>, a <fr:link href="/def-005I/" title="Map with an Inverse" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005I/" display-uri="def-005I" type="local">map with an inverse</fr:link> determines
    an <fr:link href="/def-005C/" title="Equivalence of Types" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005C/" display-uri="def-005C" type="local">equivalence</fr:link>. Explicitly, there is a canonical function
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {hasInverse}(f)\to \mathsf {isEquiv}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
 
   
   <fr:tree show-metadata="false" toc="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:taxon>Proof</fr:taxon></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(g,G,H):\mathsf {hasInverse}(f)]]></fr:tex>. By definition,
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        G:f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B,
        \qquad 
        H:g\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A.
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Therefore <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(g,G):\mathsf {sec}(f)]]></fr:tex> and
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(g,H):\mathsf {retr}(f)]]></fr:tex>. Pairing them gives
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \bigl ((g,G),(g,H)\bigr )
        :\mathsf {sec}(f)\times \mathsf {retr}(f)
        :\equiv \mathsf {isEquiv}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
 
</fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors />
                    <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0016/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>thm-0016</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/thm-0016/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="An Equivalence Has an Inverse">An Equivalence Has an Inverse</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Proposition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter><html:p>
    For every map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f:A\to  B]]></fr:tex>, an <fr:link href="/def-005C/" title="Equivalence of Types" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005C/" display-uri="def-005C" type="local">equivalence</fr:link> determines a
    <fr:link href="/def-005I/" title="Map with an Inverse" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005I/" display-uri="def-005I" type="local">map with an inverse</fr:link>. Explicitly, there is a canonical function
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathsf {isEquiv}(f)\to \mathsf {hasInverse}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
 
   
   <fr:tree show-metadata="false" toc="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:taxon>Proof</fr:taxon></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
    Suppose that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> is equipped with a section and a retraction,
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        (g,G):\mathsf {sec}(f),
        \qquad 
        (h,H):\mathsf {retr}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Thus <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g,h:B\to  A]]></fr:tex>,
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G:f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B]]></fr:tex>, and
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[H:h\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A]]></fr:tex>. First construct a homotopy
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K:g\sim  h]]></fr:tex>. For each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[y:B]]></fr:tex>, define
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        K(y)
        :\equiv 
        H(g(y))^{-1}\cdot \mathsf {ap}_h(G(y))
        :g(y)=h(y).
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Indeed, the two factors have types
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        H(g(y))^{-1}:g(y)=h(f(g(y))),
        \qquad 
        \mathsf {ap}_h(G(y)):h(f(g(y)))=h(y),
    ]]></fr:tex>
    so their concatenation is well typed.

    We now make the section <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g]]></fr:tex> into a left inverse as well. For each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x:A]]></fr:tex>,
    define
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        H_g(x)
        :\equiv 
        K(f(x))\cdot  H(x)
        :g(f(x))=x.
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Therefore <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[H_g:g\circ  f\sim \mathsf {id}_A]]></fr:tex>, while the original homotopy
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G]]></fr:tex> already gives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f\circ  g\sim \mathsf {id}_B]]></fr:tex>. Hence
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        (g,G,H_g):\mathsf {hasInverse}(f).
    ]]></fr:tex>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
 
</fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
                <html:p>
        Identity maps, composites, and inverse maps are equivalences. Equivalences satisfy
        two-out-of-three, so in
        a composite, any two of the three maps being equivalences forces the third to be one.
    </html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>7</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:title text="Identities of dependent pairs">Identities of dependent pairs</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
        For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[u=(x,a)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v=(y,b)]]></fr:tex> in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Sigma _{x:A}B(x)]]></fr:tex>, an identity consists of an
        identity in the base and a compatible identity in the fiber:
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
            (u=v)\simeq 
            \Sigma _{p:x=y}\bigl (\mathsf {transport}_B(p,a)=b\bigr ).
        ]]></fr:tex>
        This equivalence is the basic tool for calculating equality in structured objects. It also
        explains why equality of ordinary pairs is componentwise only after accounting for
        dependency.
    </html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
